metrics = {'Inertia': [], 'Silhouette': [], 'Davies-Bouldin': []}

for k in range(2, 11):
    kmeans = KMeans(n_clusters=k, random_state=42, n_init=10)
    labels = kmeans.fit_predict(X)
    
    metrics['Inertia'].append(kmeans.inertia_)
    metrics['Silhouette'].append(silhouette_score(X, labels))
    metrics['Davies-Bouldin'].append(davies_bouldin_score(X, labels))

# Plot
fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 3, figsize=(15, 4))

axes[0].plot(range(2, 11), metrics['Inertia'], 'o-')
axes[0].set_title('Inertia (Lower better)')
axes[0].set_ylabel('Inertia')

axes[1].plot(range(2, 11), metrics['Silhouette'], 'o-', color='green')
axes[1].set_title('Silhouette Score (Higher better)')
axes[1].set_ylabel('Score')

axes[2].plot(range(2, 11), metrics['Davies-Bouldin'], 'o-', color='red')
axes[2].set_title('Davies-Bouldin Index (Lower better)')
axes[2].set_ylabel('Index')

for ax in axes:
    ax.set_xlabel('k')
    ax.grid(True, alpha=0.3)

plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()

# Choose k that is good on multiple metrics
```

**Key Points:**
🔑 Inertia: Sum of squared distances within clusters
🔑 Lower inertia doesn't always mean better k
🔑 Use multiple metrics: silhouette, Davies-Bouldin
🔑 Elbow method: Look for diminishing returns
🔑 Domain knowledge: Do clusters make sense?

