labels_km = kmeans.fit_predict(X_crescent)

axes[0].scatter(X_crescent[:, 0], X_crescent[:, 1], c=labels_km, cmap='viridis')
axes[0].scatter(kmeans.cluster_centers_[:, 0],
               kmeans.cluster_centers_[:, 1],
               c='red', marker='X', s=300)
axes[0].set_title('K-Means (k=2)\nFails to find natural crescents')

# DBSCAN
dbscan = DBSCAN(eps=0.3, min_samples=5)
labels_db = dbscan.fit_predict(X_crescent)

n_clusters = len(set(labels_db)) - (1 if -1 in labels_db else 0)
axes[1].scatter(X_crescent[:, 0], X_crescent[:, 1], c=labels_db, cmap='viridis')
axes[1].set_title(f'DBSCAN\nSuccessfully finds {n_clusters} crescents')

for ax in axes:
    ax.set_aspect('equal')

plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()
```

**Key Points:**
🔑 K-Means assumes spherical clusters (minimizes distance to centroid)
🔑 DBSCAN finds density-based clusters (any shape)
🔑 For crescents/spirals: DBSCAN much better
🔑 DBSCAN naturally handles outliers
🔑 Don't assume globular shapes without checking

