## ⚖️ Comparison with Logistic Regression

### Naive Bayes vs Logistic Regression

**Both are probabilistic classifiers:**

```
Naive Bayes:
P(Y | X) = P(X | Y) × P(Y) / P(X)  (Generative)

Logistic Regression:
P(Y | X) = sigmoid(wᵀx + b)  (Discriminative)
```

**Key Differences:**

```
Naive Bayes:
✓ Learns class-conditional distributions
✓ Fast training (closed-form solution)
✓ Few parameters (linear in features)
✓ Works well with small datasets
✓ Easy to add new classes
✗ Naive independence assumption
✗ May be biased if assumption violated

Logistic Regression:
✓ No distributional assumptions
✓ More flexible decision boundaries
✓ Usually more accurate (if enough data)
✗ Slower training (iterative)
✗ More parameters
✗ Needs more data
✗ Hard to extend to multiclass naturally
```

**Code - Comparison:**
```python
from sklearn.naive_bayes import MultinomialNB
from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression
from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer
from sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score, roc_auc_score

# Prepare data
vectorizer = TfidfVectorizer(max_features=1000)
X_train_vec = vectorizer.fit_transform(X_train)
X_test_vec = vectorizer.transform(X_test)

# Naive Bayes
nb = MultinomialNB()
nb.fit(X_train_vec, y_train)
y_pred_nb = nb.predict(X_test_vec)
y_proba_nb = nb.predict_proba(X_test_vec)[:, 1]

# Logistic Regression
lr = LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000)
lr.fit(X_train_vec, y_train)
y_pred_lr = lr.predict(X_test_vec)
y_proba_lr = lr.predict_proba(X_test_vec)[:, 1]

# Compare
print("Naive Bayes:")
print(f"  Accuracy: {accuracy_score(y_test, y_pred_nb):.3f}")
print(f"  AUC-ROC: {roc_auc_score(y_test, y_proba_nb):.3f}")

print("\nLogistic Regression:")
print(f"  Accuracy: {accuracy_score(y_test, y_pred_lr):.3f}")
print(f"  AUC-ROC: {roc_auc_score(y_test, y_proba_lr):.3f}")
```

